[Music]  6 40 here on a wednesday morning and now  it is time to join us  join once again with metro sheriff joe  lombardo for our morning cup of joe's  segment sheriff good to see you once  again  good morning john good to see you well  let's get into things here  sir there's been unfortunately across  the country a surge in attacks on the  asian-american community this is  somewhat con  connected to the coronavirus how is  metro working with the  large asian population we have here in  the valley to protect them and  make sure everybody's secure  yeah unfortunately that is occurring um  but  fortunately we have not seen that here  in clark county as of yet  but to answer your question we have a  very robust uh  relationship with the asian community in  particular the asian chamber commerce  and the asian american group  and all the other affiliated uh social  groups and  political groups that across the county  acros  in particular to the chinatown area we  spent  not a significant i wouldn't use the  word significant but we spent a great  deal of our time  on south central area command spring  valley area command  addressing the asian community  so i think our relationship with them is  probably as robust as any other  demographic  but we have not seen that but we've made  them aware of the important pieces to  make them aware of it  aware of their surroundings and ensure  they don't become victimized from folks  trying to cause harm absolutely  something we do certainly don't want to  see here in the valley  get going now also metro sheriff  lombardo we cover barricade situations  frequently here in the valley in fact i  think we counted about  one a day for the last three weeks  something along those lines  so a question we have is how does metro  determine when such a big response on  these barricade situations is needed  when someone is holed up somewhere  yeah there has been you know i don't  know what the issue is recently um there  has been a frequency  of those type of events occurring here  in the uh beginning of the year  we average right around 75 80 of those  in totality year over year and what what  occurs john is  is you know it's an unknown  our patrol officer is called usually um  reference a domestic violence situation  that's usually the majority of those  type of calls  because of the emotions involved and and  people um  may be armed or or some other device  that would cause harm to the local  residents and  and through those emotional episodes  that decide on for unknown reasons  that they're going to barricade in their  house and and they're going to force the  cops to make a decision for them and so  what happens to those is a patrol  officer  shows up the situation may unfold before  them or we may have some knowledge  before we arrive  and then the subject the primary suspect  says hey  i'm not coming out come in and get me or  i'm going to wait you out and decide  what i'm going to do with my own  personal life either commit suicide or  just give up  so that requires a lot of resources to  evacuate the surrounding apartments  if you notice recently there was one  where the guy set his own apartment on  fire  which in turn has an effect on all the  surrounding  uh residences that we have to deal with  ensure that we know  who's inside those residences we get  them evacuated  and we provide them safety and if  they're armed with the firearm  you know they could discharge around  right through the walls  that have an effect on unsuspecting  people so those it's very  resource intensive but the whole key is  life safety  life safety seeing stabilization  and the preservation of property we go  through that  hierarchy when we make decisions and  swat  is our our our best tactical unit  to contain those folks and along with  swat is negotiators  so they have they tried to talk them out  and it could take eight hours or it  could take two hours and that's why it  seems overwhelming  but just on that point though when  you're talking about a situation where  that  it's a tense situation emotions are  charged up does the presence of swot and  all the officers not that it's not  necessary but does that dial up the  temperature  when maybe the real resolution might be  to try and dial it down  yeah you know what you're exactly right  but in  in the cases of the of these if you  recall in my description  we don't have all those individuals  there in the early throes of it  it's escalated to a point where swot is  necessary  in order to de-escalate the situation  and and more often than not that  overwhelming show of force  uh changes the mindset of the individual  and that individual says hey  i i can't survive this uh or i'm not  accomplishing what i want to accomplish  and it is a benefit for  us the community and themselves um and  de-escalating that situation so it's  it's  it's really a opposite of what you are  thinking  and i would agree with your thought  process but in this situation it's not  let's switch gears and talk about school  uh k through third graders and ccsd go  back on monday some of them do anyway  how's metro getting ready for the return  to kids on campus  yeah that's that's great news i wish it  was just a little more robust than that  the uh well we we have a school program  that we do every year this is in  conjunction with the clark county school  police for those of them in the las  vegas community that don't understand  the school district has their own police  department and what we are is a force  multiplier for them we come in and  assist whenever necessary  um to address the that environment but  where we mostly get involved is  in the ingress and egress of the kids at  the beginning of the day  and the end of the day we provide some  safety for the  people coming to pick up the kids  ensuring that they're not getting caught  up in traffic or causing harm in the  local businesses when they decide to  leave school  and overwhelm the stores though all  those kind of things that you could  imagine  through mischief that kids would attempt  to do  but but our role is mostly before and  after  and then the school police provide that  protection  during the school day all right let's  finally very quickly sheriff talk about  the transition las vegas is going  through trying to get  beyond covet post pandemic on the resort  corridor  how might metro adjust uh law  enforcement on the las vegas strip at  the resorts  as we move forward and as more people  start coming back to las vegas  yeah that's a that's a that's something  that we're fully aware of  and you know and we're so used to  dealing with the insurgents of crowds  with our special events and everything  else  goes along with the the community of las  vegas but in this situation we did have  some adjustment of resources in the  strip corridor and  to include fremont street um so as  as the tourism diminished we pushed a  lot of those officers out into the  surrounding neighborhoods  to deal with you know daily crime and  then as the tourism  returns and to include convention spaces  uh all those staffed individuals that we  have in convention center area command  and downtown area command  will just be brought back into the fold  and uh  it's very um fluid it's very easy  for us to make adjustments in that  aspect and and actually i'm looking  forward to it and i mean it's  as you can imagine anybody else it's  very hard for us to  adjust just like it is for a person  looking for a job  in that environment to move resources  quickly and efficiently on a you know  short notice but we're aware of it we're  we're fully  uh ready to adjust  as well i'm sure you guys are um and  we're looking forward to it i think  we're all ready for some normal sheriff  lombardo thank you very much for joining  us this morning if you at home have a  question for the sheriff  you can submit it of course eight  newsnow dot com find that if it's  answered every wednesday  6 40

